  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a small, qualitative sample of 25 women from a single city, which may not represent the experiences of older populations in other urban configurations.",
      "what": "This study analyzes the everyday mobility of 25 older women living in three central neighborhoods of Santiago, Chile, using semi-structured interviews and participatory mapping. The findings show that walking serves as the primary mode of transport for these residents, facilitating daily tasks, caregiving, and social connections with peers. Participants identified specific physical barriers in the built environment that restrict their movement, including narrow and uneven sidewalks, a lack of curb ramps, and insufficient public benches.",
      "why": "It provides empirical evidence from a Global South context on how neighborhood infrastructure directly shapes the independent mobility and social integration of older women, offering specific design targets to support aging in place."
    }
